WEBVTT

00:00:00.635 --> 00:00:24.651
<v Riley>Hey, welcome back. Today we've got a new salmon hatchery going in near Hanceville, on Tŝilhqot'in territory — and a question the reporting never answers: who actually controls the fish. Then in the Deep Dive, we're pitting a Google-backed coal-mine retrofit against tribal-owned solar in New Mexico to ask who's really self-sufficient, and who's just well connected. That and more, coming right up.

00:00:38.126 --> 00:00:58.366
<v Riley>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities — I'm Riley. It's Thursday, September 3rd, 2026. Here in the Cariboo, on the traditional and unceded territories of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations. Today's theme is Indigenous Lands &amp; Innovation.

00:00:59.366 --> 00:01:07.574
<v Casey>And I'm Casey. Today we're looking at where Indigenous nations set the terms on land and technology, and where those terms get set for them instead.

00:01:08.274 --> 00:01:25.746
<v Riley>Quick weather note before we get going. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning it's overcast, around 13 degrees, with a high of 16 and a low of 11. Expect slight rain most of the day — close to a hundred percent chance of precipitation, about eight millimetres total by evening.

00:01:26.526 --> 00:01:37.998
<v Casey>Around the rest of the region — 100 Mile House sitting near 10 with light drizzle, Williams Lake and Quesnel both around 13 and overcast, Hanceville around 12. Grab a jacket before you head out.

00:01:38.198 --> 00:01:38.982
<v Riley>Let's get into it.

00:01:40.382 --> 00:02:01.646
<v Riley>Eight stories close to home today, plus a wildfire recovery note, an election announcement, and a fisheries project big enough to shape the region for years. Let's start there. West of Williams Lake, near Hanceville, a permanent conservation hatchery is going in to rebuild salmon populations that have been hit hard by years of drought and low water.

00:02:01.546 --> 00:02:03.690
<v Casey>Hanceville — that's Tŝilhqot'in territory.

00:02:05.128 --> 00:02:33.408
<v Riley>It is. The Williams Lake Tribune reports the new facility replaces what had been a temporary setup, giving fisheries managers a year-round operation instead of scrambling every spawning season to rebuild infrastructure from scratch. What the published reporting doesn't spell out is who actually holds decision-making authority over which fish get selected for brood stock, what the release targets are, or how that run-strength data gets shared once it's collected.

00:02:34.669 --> 00:02:45.757
<v Casey>A permanent facility on Tŝilhqot'in territory is real progress for the fishery. Whether it's permanent progress for Tŝilhqot'in governance over that fishery is a separate question the article doesn't answer.

00:02:47.178 --> 00:03:01.138
<v Riley>We'll come back to that later in the show. The Quesnel Fire Department knocked down a wildland fire in the South Hills area Tuesday evening — My Cariboo Now reports no structures were lost and the fire was contained the same night it started.

00:03:02.374 --> 00:03:20.286
<v Casey>Quesnel's mayoral race got its first big name entrant this week, too. Former city councillor and provincial MLA Coralee Oakes announced Wednesday she's running for mayor in the October 17th election, with a campaign built around public safety, economic development, workforce planning, and preparing the city for future growth.

00:03:21.485 --> 00:03:33.765
<v Riley>Staying with fire season — Williams Lake lifted its campfire ban within city limits as of Wednesday, according to the Tribune. A sign the drought pressure that's dogged the region since spring is finally easing.

00:03:33.615 --> 00:03:49.423
<v Casey>The 100 Mile Free Press outdoors column put it about as plainly as you can this week. The writer says the last of the hot days of summer look to be gone, taking with them the threat of a major forest fire and the smoky conditions that have hung over the region since June.

00:03:50.687 --> 00:04:00.039
<v Riley>That columnist also had a boat mishap worth mentioning — he lost the key fob for his motor mid-lake and was stuck rowing back. Said at least the oars don't need a battery.

00:04:01.386 --> 00:04:05.754
<v Casey>Sometimes the low-tech option is the one that actually works when the electronics fail.

00:04:06.554 --> 00:04:30.138
<v Riley>On the health front, Statistics Canada is bringing its Canadian Health Measures Survey to Quesnel this fall. The Cariboo Observer reports interviews and physical measurements will run from October 21st through November 24th. It's a rotating national survey, and households get selected essentially at random — the data collected feeds federal health policy modelling for years.

00:04:31.504 --> 00:04:38.504
<v Casey>If a StatsCan letter shows up in your mailbox this fall, it's real. Worth knowing before you assume it's a scam and toss it.

00:04:39.704 --> 00:04:58.288
<v Riley>Provincial politics had a rough week too. BC Conservative Leader Kerry-Lynne Findlay rejected what she called "baseless" allegations of legal or ethical wrongdoing on Wednesday. My Cariboo Now reports she fired her chief of staff and named a replacement in the same statement, all while two more MLAs left her caucus.

00:04:59.438 --> 00:05:06.022
<v Casey>That's three caucus departures in a matter of weeks now. Something's bleeding from that party, and it isn't just messaging.

00:05:07.512 --> 00:05:26.416
<v Riley>Related — the Vancouver Sun reports RCMP have now interviewed former BC Conservative MLA Ian Paton over his claim that someone offered him a paid agricultural adviser role, at a salary matching his MLA pay, in exchange for resigning his seat. Paton says the RCMP appeared to take the allegation seriously.

00:05:27.523 --> 00:05:32.515
<v Casey>So the party's losing members and now facing a police interview about how it's losing them.

00:05:33.921 --> 00:05:46.369
<v Riley>One more item, from just outside our region — Nelson's WildSafeBC coordinator, Lisa Thomson, stepped down this week after a string of grizzly bear deaths in her area. The Tribune's report doesn't name a successor yet.

00:05:47.569 --> 00:06:06.937
<v Casey>On the tech side — Amazon rolled out a feature this week letting customers use its AI assistant to check whether an email, text, or phone call claiming to be from the company is actually legitimate. The Verge reports it's aimed squarely at impersonation scams, where fraudsters spoof Amazon branding to get people to hand over payment details.

00:06:08.157 --> 00:06:14.157
<v Riley>Useful, assuming you trust the company that keeps getting impersonated to also be the one verifying itself.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:38.900
<v Casey>Bigger story, and not ours to weigh in on directly — Chevron confirmed this week it's expanding operations in Venezuela. Fast Company reports the joint venture is worth more than seven billion dollars over five years, aiming to more than double production to around six hundred thousand barrels a day, following a deal the Trump administration struck days earlier with opposition figures in Venezuela's government.

00:06:40.361 --> 00:06:50.009
<v Riley>Pure US foreign policy call, nothing Canadian listeners get a vote on. But it's a precedent worth watching for how fast oil majors move once a political door opens.

00:06:51.271 --> 00:07:05.895
<v Casey>Mother Jones had a piece this week on political organizing across southern US states, centred on Tennessee representative Justin Pearson, who first drew national attention after being expelled from his state legislature for demonstrating in support of gun reform.

00:07:07.246 --> 00:07:22.566
<v Riley>CNET covered something with a closer echo for us — at the G20 summit, protests over AI data centre expansion followed tech leaders right to the meeting's doorstep. Communities near proposed data centre sites are pushing back hard on water use and power draw.

00:07:24.022 --> 00:07:29.422
<v Casey>That's the same fight showing up everywhere a hyperscaler wants to build. We'll touch that again in the Deep Dive.

00:07:30.730 --> 00:07:50.794
<v Riley>A Russian national is facing up to twenty years in prison after extradition on charges tied to a phishing campaign that allegedly infected eighty thousand PCs across the US, according to Tom's Hardware. Prosecutors say the campaign harvested login credentials and personal data through remote access tools installed on victims' machines.

00:07:50.694 --> 00:07:57.774
<v Casey>Twenty years for stolen passwords sounds steep, until you remember eighty thousand machines is a small city's worth of computers.

00:07:58.974 --> 00:08:16.142
<v Riley>Last story for today, and it's worth the full telling. TechRadar reports more than five thousand Dropbox accounts were compromised after a bug in Lenovo's identity verification system let attackers walk in without ever guessing a password — an email address alone was enough to trigger the exploit.

00:08:17.358 --> 00:08:23.006
<v Casey>How does a Lenovo bug end up compromising Dropbox accounts? Those are two completely different companies.

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:50.271
<v Riley>Lenovo's ID verification service was apparently used as a backend check by Dropbox's own authentication flow — one of those quiet integrations most users never know exists until it breaks. The breach window ran from August 4th to the 21st. Most of the affected accounts didn't have two-factor authentication turned on, and in roughly a third of them, attackers actually viewed or downloaded stored documents.

00:08:51.620 --> 00:09:06.764
<v Casey>The bug's fixed now, but the lesson isn't really about Lenovo or Dropbox specifically. Identity verification gets bolted onto services after the fact, through partnerships most customers never see, and that seam between two companies' software is exactly where things fall through.

00:09:07.986 --> 00:09:13.066
<v Riley>Which is as good a note as any to close the roundup on before we get to today's spotlight.

00:09:14.466 --> 00:09:40.210
<v Casey>Today's spotlight is on the Cariboo Chilcotin Aboriginal Training Employment Centre — CCATEC for short. They connect First Nations job seekers across the region with labour-market training built around actual openings: trades, health care, natural resources, whatever the local economy needs that quarter. They work directly with employers to design training around real hiring, rather than handing out generic certificates nobody's actually recruiting for.

00:09:40.777 --> 00:09:49.233
<v Riley>That employer-matched approach is the piece that keeps rural workforce programs from producing a credential nobody can use once the ceremony's over.

00:09:49.772 --> 00:10:07.308
<v Casey>You can find them at ccatec dot com if you're a job seeker or an employer looking to partner with them. Which is a decent bridge into today's Deep Dive, honestly. Training and employment is one kind of self-determination. Today we're looking at another — who owns the actual infrastructure.

00:10:08.708 --> 00:10:27.404
<v Riley>There's a story out this week about a clean-energy project going into an old West Virginia strip mine. Google-backed, pairing solar with a new zinc battery chemistry from a company called Eos, on five hundred acres that have sat idle for twenty years. Reads well on paper — coal scar becomes clean power for the grid.

00:10:27.956 --> 00:10:43.876
<v Casey>It's a good decarbonization story. It is not an Indigenous story. Nothing in the reporting ties it to tribal land, tribal partnership, or tribal decision-making of any kind. It's a utility and a tech company doing a corporate retrofit on land nobody's disputing ownership of anymore.

00:10:44.502 --> 00:11:05.846
<v Riley>Which is the question worth asking this week. Is the win here self-sufficiency, or is it who's plugged into the right network? That West Virginia site only exists because Google needed grid capacity nearby and the utility needed dispatchable storage close to where demand already sits. It's a connected project wearing the costume of land reclamation.

00:11:06.391 --> 00:11:20.559
<v Casey>And that's the trap in calling every old-extraction-site retrofit an innovation story. Reused land isn't the same as land under Indigenous control. If we're talking about energy sovereignty today, the sharper test case isn't a former coal lease in Appalachia.

00:11:21.178 --> 00:11:43.706
<v Riley>It's the tribal-led projects. NPR reported this year on a utility-scale solar-plus-battery installation brought online on tribal land in New Mexico. Native Renewables has been running off-grid solar installs for Navajo and Hopi households that never had grid power to begin with — not a retrofit, a first connection, built and owned on the Nation's own terms from the start.

00:11:44.286 --> 00:11:59.326
<v Casey>Those are different animals. One's a Nation deciding what gets built on land it already controls. The other's a former coal company's lease getting a second life because Google needed storage capacity somewhere, and West Virginia happened to have the acreage and the interconnect already in place.

00:11:59.847 --> 00:12:11.999
<v Riley>So — are those Navajo and Hopi off-grid systems self-sufficient, or connected in a different sense? Connected to Native Renewables' financing, connected to solar hardware manufactured somewhere else.

00:12:12.596 --> 00:12:30.108
<v Casey>Both. A household owning the panels on its own roof is self-sufficient in the way that matters most day to day — nobody outside the community can flip the switch off or dictate the terms of use. But the hardware still comes out of a global supply chain. Nobody's manufacturing solar cells on the reservation.

00:12:30.647 --> 00:12:43.703
<v Riley>And nobody's mining silicon out near Hanceville either. Self-sufficiency was never about building every component from scratch. It's about who holds the decision that matters — whether the power stays on, and who has the authority to turn it off.

00:12:44.249 --> 00:13:00.201
<v Casey>That framing has its own risk, though. Programs like Native Renewables run largely on grant cycles and philanthropic funding, as publicly reported. If that funding tightens, the panels stay bolted to the roof, but the maintenance contracts and replacement parts don't necessarily keep following.

00:13:00.811 --> 00:13:18.083
<v Riley>That's a real constraint, but a different failure mode than the alternative. A funding gap is a problem you solve by finding a different funder. A grid that was never built out to those homes at all is a problem you can't solve by writing a check — someone still has to run the line.

00:13:18.583 --> 00:13:38.463
<v Casey>Fine. But look at the flip side, because it's the sharper counterexample to any story claiming clean innovation automatically helps land-connected communities. The Guardian reported this week that Barclays faces a formal complaint over its financing of the Rampal coal plant in Bangladesh, built right next to the Sundarbans — the largest mangrove forest on Earth.

00:13:38.807 --> 00:13:42.935
<v Riley>Two million people depend on that forest for fisheries and forestry work.

00:13:43.467 --> 00:14:01.507
<v Casey>And the complaint alleges the plant's pollution and erosion risks threaten exactly that livelihood base. This is what happens when the capital and the decision-making sit somewhere else — a bank office in London. The people whose land and water are actually on the line don't get a vote on whether that financing goes through.

00:14:02.059 --> 00:14:23.675
<v Riley>Which is the strongest argument for the New Mexico and Navajo models, not against them. When a Nation owns the asset outright, a bank in a different hemisphere can't unilaterally decide a project's worth the risk to somebody else's mangrove forest. Rampal is what happens when "connected" means connected to capital instead of connected to the land the project sits on.

00:14:24.301 --> 00:14:48.685
<v Casey>Don't let that tip into "Indigenous-owned, therefore automatically safe." Ownership changes who's accountable when something goes wrong. It doesn't eliminate the tension between a project's economics and its environmental footprint. A tribally owned solar farm can still sign a bad interconnection deal that reads like the Rampal financing reads here — outside capital writing the terms because the Nation needs that capital to move at all.

00:14:49.285 --> 00:14:57.445
<v Riley>That's the real leverage question, then. Not self-sufficient versus connected as a binary — which kind of connection, and who wrote the contract.

00:14:58.071 --> 00:15:14.631
<v Casey>Right. And that's where the Cariboo piece of this stops being theoretical. It loops straight back to the hatchery story from this hour — a permanent conservation hatchery going in near Hanceville, on Tŝilhqot'in territory, rebuilding salmon runs that drought and habitat loss have hammered for years.

00:15:15.154 --> 00:15:38.666
<v Riley>The published reporting doesn't say who holds decision-making authority over brood stock selection, release targets, or how the run-strength data gets shared once it's collected. Not a knock on the project itself. A permanent facility instead of a temporary one is real progress — but permanent for whom, and accountable to whom, are two separate questions the article leaves open.

00:15:39.345 --> 00:15:59.865
<v Casey>If the Nation holds a seat on brood stock decisions and gets the run-strength data directly, rather than through a provincial summary released six months later, that's closer to the Navajo solar model. If it's a provincial or federal facility that happens to sit on Tŝilhqot'in land and reports back on its own schedule, that's closer to Rampal with better intentions attached.

00:16:00.188 --> 00:16:03.532
<v Riley>We don't have enough in what's published to know which one this is.

00:16:04.163 --> 00:16:24.931
<v Casey>There's a version of this worth trying that doesn't require waiting on that answer, though. If a Cariboo Nation wanted to test the ownership question on a smaller scale than a hatchery, a community-scale solar-plus-storage pilot on band-owned land — sized for a school or a health centre, not a whole grid — would answer the "who decides" question inside months, not years.

00:16:25.513 --> 00:16:49.353
<v Riley>That's the kind of thing BC Hydro's community and Indigenous partnership programs claim to fund, at least on paper. The real test isn't the grant application. It's whether the interconnection agreement lets the Nation walk away from BC Hydro's terms later, or whether it locks them into a twenty-year contract written entirely by someone else's lawyers, in print small enough to need a magnifying glass.

00:16:49.973 --> 00:16:58.797
<v Casey>Which is worth reading line by line before signing, and worth raising at a band council meeting before a project like that gets announced, not after the ribbon-cutting.

00:16:59.447 --> 00:17:30.575
<v Riley>There's a data-centre echo here too, since it showed up twice today. The G20 protests over AI data centre expansion are about the same underlying question — communities objecting to water and power draw from projects they had no say in siting. A rural Nation weighing a data centre lease on its land would be negotiating from the same position Bangladesh's fisheries communities were never given: a seat at the table before the contract's signed, not a complaint process after construction starts.

00:17:30.975 --> 00:17:44.823
<v Casey>So — self-sufficient or well-connected. It doesn't resolve cleanly, and maybe it isn't supposed to. West Virginia is connected and calls itself clean. New Mexico is connected too, just to a different network — one the Nation can actually renegotiate.

00:17:45.427 --> 00:17:59.875
<v Riley>The difference was never independence versus dependence. It's whether you can walk away from the deal you're in. That's what I'd want the hatchery reporting to fill in next — not whether the technology works, but who has the standing to say no to it.

00:18:00.520 --> 00:18:12.984
<v Casey>If anyone listening has the actual governance details on that hatchery project, or wants to point us to the interconnection terms on any of these energy deals, send it our way — feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a. We'd like to know.

00:18:13.305 --> 00:18:16.577
<v Riley>That's it for today — thanks for spending part of your morning with us.

00:18:17.120 --> 00:18:22.552
<v Casey>Have a great rest of your day. And try not to lose your boat key like that guy in the outdoors column.

00:18:22.652 --> 00:18:40.404
<v Casey>Look, I'll be straight with you — we didn't get First Nations comms folks on the line for this one, and that's a gap we should fill. If you're working in Indigenous communications out here in the Cariboo, we'd genuinely like to hear what's actually happening on the ground, not just what sounds good in a press release.